# APZHUB Step 5 — Access model UI (locked)

This is the **core of APZHUB**. Design the **admin interaction model for access control**, not just data tables.

If this is clean, everything else becomes easy. If messy, the platform fails.

## 5.1 Core principle

Access must be:

- **Visual**  
- **Traceable**  
- **Easy to assign**  
- **Easy to override**  
- **Impossible to misunderstand**  

Instead of scattered forms:

👉 **Matrix + Inspector + Bundles + Overrides**

## 5.2 Admin access domains (left rail)

Under Admin, access breaks into distinct purposes — **no overlap**:

- Users  
- Access Matrix  
- Bundles  
- Services  
- Provisioning is **separate** but **linked**

## 5.3 Users view (entry point)

### Main canvas — Users list

Table columns e.g.:

- Name, Email  
- Platform Role  
- Status (active/inactive)  
- Linked accounts  
- Last login  
- Access summary (compact)  
- Flags (issues, conflicts)  

### Selecting a user

👉 Right panel = **User Access Inspector**

## 5.3.1 User Access Inspector (critical)

### Section A: Platform role

- Dropdown selector  
- Immediate update  

### Section B: Access bundle(s)

- Assigned bundles  
- Add/remove bundle  
- Expandable: what each bundle grants  

### Section C: Service access list

Table or grouped list. Columns:

| Service     | Access   | Source            | Status |
| ----------- | -------- | ----------------- | ------ |
| (per row)   | effective role | Bundle / Manual override / System default | Provisioned / Pending / Failed |

- **Access** = effective role  
- **Source:** Bundle, Manual override, System default  
- **Status:** Provisioned, Pending, Failed  

### Section D: Override control

Admin can: change role per service, remove access, add access.

Overrides must be clearly marked: **“Manual override”**.

### Section E: Linked accounts

- Google connected / not (future: Microsoft, etc.)  

### Section F: Actions

- Re-provision user  
- Remove all access  
- Lock user  
- Reset sessions  

## 5.4 Access Matrix view (power feature)

**Most important admin tool** — full system overview.

### 5.4.1 Layout

- **Rows:** Users  
- **Columns:** Services  
- **Cells:** Role assignment  

### 5.4.2 Cell behavior

Each cell must:

- Show role clearly  
- Color-code by level  
- Be clickable to edit  
- Show source (bundle/manual)  

Click → inline selector or inspector panel.

### 5.4.3 Filters

Support: by service, by role, by bundle, by user group; **only conflicts**; **only overrides**.

### 5.4.4 Use cases

Audit access system-wide, detect inconsistencies, mass-adjust roles, validate provisioning logic.

## 5.5 Bundles UI

Bundles scale assignment.

### 5.5.1 Bundle list

Table: Bundle name, Description, Services covered, Number of users, Last updated.

### 5.5.2 Bundle editor (main canvas)

**Section A — Metadata:** name, description.

**Section B — Service role assignments:** per-service role table (Service | Role).

**Section C — Preview:** “This bundle gives:” — full resulting access list.

**Section D — Users using bundle:** list + impact awareness.

### 5.5.3 Behavior

When bundle changes: users **inherit** updates **unless overridden**. Overrides **remain intact**.

## 5.6 Services UI

Defines how each system behaves.

### 5.6.1 Service list

Name, Type (internal/external), Auth type (OIDC / SAML / Vault / API / DB), Status, Provisioning method.

### 5.6.2 Service detail view

**Section A — General:** name, description, category.

**Section B — Role mapping:** APZHUB role → service role (e.g. Viewer → read-only, Editor → contributor, Admin → admin).

**Section C — Provisioning method:** API, SCIM, DB safe, Manual, Vault.

**Section D — Health:** last sync, errors.

## 5.7 Provisioning integration (linked UI)

From any access change:

- Provisioning job triggers  
- Visible in provisioning queue  
- Status reflected in user/service views  

**Automatic and transparent.**

## 5.8 Visual rules

- Consistent **role colors**  
- **Source** (bundle/manual) always visible  
- **Status** always visible  
- **No hidden logic**  

## 5.9 Interaction flows

**Assigning access to a user:** Open user → assign bundle → override if needed → save → system provisions automatically → status updates live.

**Fixing an issue:** Alert → open user/service → see failed provisioning → fix role or retry → system updates.

**Auditing access:** Open matrix → filter → inspect inconsistencies → adjust inline.

## 5.10 What makes this powerful

Clarity (matrix), speed (bundles), flexibility (overrides), control (mapping), visibility (status + audit) — design for **all five**.

## Locked Step 5 statement

**APZHUB access control is managed through a structured UI consisting of a user-centric inspector, a system-wide access matrix, reusable bundles, service-level role mapping, and automatic provisioning with visible status, allowing clear assignment, override, and audit of per-service roles for each user.**
